WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.302
Hi, I'm Bramble!

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:05.674
On one benchmark row, Jev got zero of a hundred.

00:00:05.674 --> 00:00:06.686
Not low: zero.

00:00:06.686 --> 00:00:09.046
Here's the mechanism that makes that happen.

00:00:11.142 --> 00:00:12.094
Here's the idea.

00:00:12.094 --> 00:00:15.904
If every option is scored alone, an option that points at another

00:00:15.904 --> 00:00:17.174
is pointing at nothing.

00:00:17.174 --> 00:00:18.444
Zero isn't bad luck.

00:00:18.444 --> 00:00:20.348
It's the signature of a structure.

00:00:21.883 --> 00:00:25.005
Take one case: the customer wants their Reno address.

00:00:25.005 --> 00:00:29.168
The scorer runs one pass per option, each holding the question, the

00:00:29.168 --> 00:00:30.903
evidence, and exactly one option.

00:00:31.323 --> 00:00:34.542
Option B says: ship to the address in option D.

00:00:34.542 --> 00:00:36.795
But pass B never contains option D.

00:00:36.795 --> 00:00:38.404
Its pointer resolves to nothing.

00:00:38.824 --> 00:00:43.653
Pass A reads as a clean shipping instruction that the evidence partly supports.

00:00:43.653 --> 00:00:45.510
It's the locally plausible answer.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:49.049
The options meet only at the softmax, as single numbers.

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:52.168
A wins every time, and A is wrong every time.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.001
Draw it as a grid.

00:00:55.001 --> 00:00:57.702
A per-option scorer lets each option see only itself.

00:00:57.702 --> 00:00:59.804
That's the shape Jev's zero points to.

00:01:00.224 --> 00:01:04.456
Laya puts every option in one sequence, each behind a mask marker,

00:01:04.456 --> 00:01:05.866
in a bidirectional encoder.

00:01:05.866 --> 00:01:07.982
Every option can see every other.

00:01:09.425 --> 00:01:13.566
On a hundred relational cases, a four-bit Qwen that reads everything

00:01:13.566 --> 00:01:15.072
at once gets fifty-three.

00:01:15.072 --> 00:01:16.201
Laya gets eight.

00:01:16.201 --> 00:01:17.331
Jev gets zero.

00:01:17.331 --> 00:01:19.213
Three-way guessing would average thirty-three.

00:01:20.558 --> 00:01:24.140
Zero is one rule, applied a hundred times, to a window that

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:25.334
never contained the answer.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.142
To recap: one pass per option, pointers that point at nothing, and

00:01:32.142 --> 00:01:34.013
a zero that reveals the shape.

00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:36.569
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:36.569 --> 00:01:37.179
I'm Bramble.

00:01:37.179 --> 00:01:37.484
Bye!
